# ─────────────────────────── Required ───────────────────────────

# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls (node-1, primary).
# The server refuses to boot without it rather than failing opaquely on the first live call.
# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.
GEMINI_API_KEY="MY_GEMINI_API_KEY"

# ADDITIONAL_GEMINI_KEYS: Optional comma-separated backup API keys for clustering & instant zero-downtime failover.
# Format: KEY_2,KEY_3,KEY_4
ADDITIONAL_GEMINI_KEYS=""

# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.
APP_URL="MY_APP_URL"

# ───────────────────────── Models (optional) ─────────────────────────

# Override the model identifiers without a code change.
# AURA_LIVE_MODEL="gemini-3.8-live"
# AURA_TTS_MODEL="gemini-3.8-flash-lite-tts"

# How long the relay waits for the provider's `setupComplete` before sending the opening
# greeting anyway. Raise it for a cold, slow project; lower it if you would rather fail
# fast than risk a delayed greeting.
# AURA_SETUP_READY_TIMEOUT_MS="8000"

# ─────────────────── Live relay security (recommended) ───────────────

# AURA_LIVE_TOKEN: Shared secret (minimum 16 characters) required on every /live upgrade.
# When unset, the relay is same-origin only, which still permits direct socket clients.
# Set it in both the Vercel project environment and the front-end build environment.
# AURA_LIVE_TOKEN="generate-a-long-random-string"

# AURA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS: Extra comma-separated origins permitted to open /live, for a
# front end hosted on a different domain. Same-origin is always allowed.
# AURA_ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://app.example.com,https://admin.example.com"

# AURA_ALLOW_MISSING_ORIGIN: Set to "true" only for non-browser clients that cannot send
# an Origin header. Leave unset in production; it disables the cross-site protection.
# AURA_ALLOW_MISSING_ORIGIN="false"

# ─────────────── Business tool providers (optional) ───────────────

# sendConfirmation returns an honest UNAVAILABLE failure when no provider is configured;
# it will never claim an email was sent. Set these to enable the capability for real.
# AURA_CONFIRMATION_WEBHOOK_URL="https://api.example.com/send-confirmation"
# AURA_TRANSFER_WEBHOOK_URL="https://api.example.com/transfer-call"

# ─────────────── Gemini capacity (single key is fine) ───────────────

# GEMINI_API_KEY alone is enough. AURA_LIVE_TOKEN and the webhook URLs above are the only
# other things you need, and all of those are optional.
#
# Gemini rate limits are enforced per Google Cloud PROJECT, not per API key. Keys that share a
# project draw from one bucket, so they add nothing. The pool detects byte-identical keys and
# collapses them into a single slot rather than pretending the capacity multiplied.
#
# To genuinely scale, use keys from SEPARATE Google Cloud projects (separate accounts work
# too). Give each one its own variable:
#
#   GEMINI_PROJECT_BETA_KEY=AQ....
#   GEMINI_PROJECT_BETA_ID=my-second-google-cloud-project
#
# The _ID variables are only used for human-readable reporting; the key carries the quota.
# Before relying on a second key, confirm it can actually serve live traffic, not just
# authenticate: run `npm run verify:keys`, which opens a real WebSocket session per key. A key
# can list models while its project lacks Live API access, and the relay would then route
# callers to a credential that cannot open a session.

# AURA_PROJECT_CAPACITY: Concurrent live sessions admitted PER Google Cloud project, so the
# pool admits up to (distinct projects x this value) in total. This is admission control, not
# a measurement. Default 4.
# AURA_PROJECT_CAPACITY="4"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────���──────────────────── Session & persistence ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# AURA_SESSION_TTL_MS: How long a call stays resumable after its last activity, so a browser
# refresh or a dropped transport resumes the same greeted call instead of a fresh one.
# Default 300000 (five minutes). This is the product's memory window.
# AURA_SESSION_TTL_MS="300000"

# AURA_DATA_DIR: Where the session, appointment, and tool-operation journals are written.
# Must be a durable path. It is instance-local, which is only safe on a single instance.
# AURA_DATA_DIR=".data"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────���───────── Hosting topology (read this before Vercel) ─────────────────────────────

# This project is correct as a SINGLE instance. The journals above are authoritative only within
# one process, because deciding "this slot is free" and claiming it must be one atomic
# read-modify-write. Two instances that cannot see each other will each accept the same 14:00
# booking and tell two customers they hold it.
#
# On Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages, or Lambda the filesystem is ephemeral and requests are
# spread across many instances, so the relay detects that and REFUSES to report ready:
#   GET /ready -> 503, persistence.topology = "MULTI_INSTANCE_RISK"
# That is deliberate. A deployment that reports ready while able to double-book is worse than
# one that refuses traffic.
#
# To run on such a platform you must either:
#   (a) provide a shared transactional store - the five properties it must satisfy are written
#       out in src/persistence/PersistenceTopology.ts, and a plain GET/SET pair cannot meet them;
#       or
#   (b) accept duplicate bookings deliberately, with the flag below. Read the warning first.

# AURA_ACK_MULTI_INSTANCE_PERSISTENCE: Set to "true" to accept that duplicate bookings are
# possible and let readiness pass. This is a decision an operator makes, not a default, so it
# cannot be enabled by accident. Prefer the single-instance container for real traffic.
# AURA_ACK_MULTI_INSTANCE_PERSISTENCE="false"

# AURA_REQUIRE_SHARED_STORE: The strict form. When "true", the acknowledgement escape hatch is
# removed and readiness stays 503 even if it is set - for a deployment that genuinely must not
# double-book, rather than merely being warned about it.
# AURA_REQUIRE_SHARED_STORE="false"

# AURA_PERSISTENCE_TOPOLOGY: Override platform detection when it cannot infer the topology.
# Values: SINGLE_INSTANCE | MULTI_INSTANCE
# AURA_PERSISTENCE_TOPOLOGY="SINGLE_INSTANCE"

# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── Deployment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# npm run dev / npm start       - long-lived Node process, calls httpServer.listen, owns the
#                                 journal. This is the single-instance deployment.
# docker build -t aura . && docker run -p 3000:3000 --env-file .env aura
#                               - the recommended production deployment: one process, one
#                                 authoritative journal, no connection duration cap.
# vercel                         - see the deployment notes in AURA-ARCHITECTURE-AUDIT.md and
#                                 vercel.json. AURA_VERCEL_HOST is set automatically by the
#                                 platform; the route is api/index.ts, and /live needs Fluid
#                                 compute enabled.
